{
  "raw": {
    "https://minnstate.wd115.myworkdayjobs.com/Minnesota_State_Careers/job/St-Cloud/Assistant-Associate-Professor-of-Counselor-Education--State-University-Faculty_JR0000005883": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Counselor Education -State University Faculty",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-12\nFull time\n\nAll Job Postings will close at 12:01 a.m. CT on the specified Closing Date (if designated). Working Title: Assistant/Associate Professor of Counselor Education -State University Faculty Institution: St. Cloud State University Classification Title: State University Faculty Bargaining Unit / Union: 209: Inter Faculty Organization City: St. Cloud FLSA: Job Exempt Full Time / Part Time: Full time Employment Condition: Unclassified - Unlimited Academic Salary Range: $64,963.00 - $193,146.00 Job Description Minnesota needs clinical mental health counselors and school counselors, and this program prepares them.  We are looking for a counselor educator who wants real influence over how that happens.      The Clinical Mental Health Counseling and School Counseling programs at St. Cloud State University invite applications for a tenure-track Assistant or Associate Professor of Counselor Education, beginning August 16th, 2027.       We are a small faculty running daytime cohort programs with our own on-campus training clinic, live supervision, and a set of community partnerships that are already operating and open to a new colleague’s ideas.  Because the faculty is small, your voice on curriculum, clinical training, and program direction will matter from your first semester – not your seventh year.     We are open on specialty. We are seeking an excellent counselor who can contribute broadly across the CACREP core curriculum, not a candidate in any single subfield. Your particular clinical and scholarly expertise – whatever it is – will help determine what we build next.    How teaching works here.     A 3/3 graduate load . Graduate courses are weighted at 4 credits in the 24-credit IFO academic year-some clinical courses at five -, so a full graduate load is 6 courses per year rather than 8.    Daytime only. Our programs run on a cohort model with daytime classes.  Students know their full schedule from admission to graduation, and so do you. In counselor education, where evening teaching is the norm, this is unusual.    No faculty member owns a course. Faculty submit teaching preferences annually. If you want to teach a course several years running to develop it, you can.  If you want to move into something new, you can. We deliberately distribute the first-year content sequence – the heaviest teaching in the program, with intensive skills feedback and the beginning of gatekeeping – across the whole faculty rather than concentrating it on newer colleagues.    You follow a cohort through its full three-year life cycle.   You teach students in their first year, again in practicum, and through internship to graduation.  Most programs don’t work this way, and it takes some getting  used to. We do it because when you make a gatekeeping decision or write a recommendation, you’re drawing on three years of watching someone develop rather than one semester’s impression.     A cohort’s first year typically includes Introduction to the profession, Ethics, Assessment in Counseling, Multicultural Counseling, and Counseling Procedures/Skills and either diagnosis and treatment planning or child/adolescent counseling.    Summer is optional, and it’s yours.   We poll students at orientation about which electives they want, and together with our advisory board build a 3-year summer rotation from that data.  Faculty teach in their own areas of interest, so this is where specialization lives – the core curriculum is taught by everyone, and the summer sequence is where your expertise becomes curriculum. Because the rotation is built from student demand and planned three years out, you’ll know your summer teaching well in advance.  Additional summer sections are also regularly available in our undergraduate program.  Summer teaching is compensated separately from the academic year contract under Article 13 of the IFO agreement.      Clinical training   Students complete practicum on campus in the College of Education, Health & Human Sciences training clinic, with live bug-in-the-ear supervision.  You’ll supervise in real time rather than from recordings – and you’ll have a clinical setting available for your own scholarship.    Students who want to specialize working with children and adolescents also have the opportunity for a community- partner practicum placement.    Faculty and students work across a set of interdisciplinary collaborations that are already running, including integrated behavioral health initiatives, support for first-year nursing students, programming for students' parents, support for international students, support for the campus counseling center, support for campus/community serving autism clinic and for campus/clinic communication/sciences disorder clinic, and youth programming in partnership with the YMCA.  You would join active partnerships rather than build from zero.    Scholarship     Our tenure process is outlined in the IFO contract.      Our training clinic, our community partnerships, and our three-year cohort structure are all available as research settings.  We support applied and community-engaged scholarship alongside traditional peer-reviewed work, and we treat dissertation-derived publication as legitimate early scholarship.       Faculty receive professional development funding under the IFO contract, are eligible for sabbatical, and have access to a range of internal grant programs.     Why this position is different.     Your workload has been thought about.   A 3/3 load.  Daytime teaching only.  Course assignment by annual preference, with no one owning a course.  An optional summer sequence in your own area of interest.    Your influence is proportional to our size.   On a faculty of 6, a new colleague’s ideas about curriculum and clinical training get implemented rather than queued.  You will shape this program from your first year, not your seventh year.      Your specialty gets six credits of its own.   Whatever you bring – trauma, addictions, couples and family work, career, group work, integrated behavioral health, counseling children and adolescents, or something we don’t currently offer – there’s a place to build it into curriculum.    Our graduates fill a real regional need.   Central Minnesota’s behavioral health workforce depends on programs like ours.      Our program at a glance   Accreditation: CACREP-accredited on 2024 standards.    Model: Cohort-based, daytime, on-campus master’s programs in CMHC and School Counseling  Cohort size:   [#] has grown equal with growth in faculty hire.    Faculty: [6] Counselor faculty, with expertise in [Social Justice and Cultural fluency, addictions, group work, complex trauma]  Student outcomes: Link to our last CAPER  Home: Department of Behavioral Health and Counseling, School of Education, Health and Human Sciences.  Degrees:   Master’s-level CMHC, School Counseling, Graduate Certificate in Addictions (non CACREP) and upcoming 3+3.  We do not offer a doctorate in counselor education at this time; our faculty teach and advise at the master’s level.  Our program's trajectory We turn away qualified applicants every year.   Our graduate program receives more applications than we have seats and has for 10 years running.   We sit in a college built around exactly this kind of work.   The College of Education, Health & Human Sciences brings education, health, and human sciences together under one roof with an emphasis on field-based learning and alignment with regional workforce needs.   The infrastructure is built. Our on-campus training clinic with live supervision, our community partnerships, and our clinical placement network are established and operating. Living here St. Cloud is a city of about 70,000 on the Mississippi River, roughly 60 miles northwest of Minneapolis-St. Paul.  Housing costs are a fraction of metro rates and commutes are measured in minutes, so a faculty salary goes considerably further here than in the Twin Cities or on either coast.  The region has a substantial healthcare and behavioral health sector, which matters if you are relocating with a partner in the field.  SCSU also operates a campus in the Twin Cities metro, if a metro-area residence suits you better.   *Employment for this position is covered by the collective bargaining agreement for the Inter Faculty Organization (IFO), which can be found at:  https://www.ifo.org/contract   Salary: *The salary range is the contractual range; however, salary upon hire is dependent upon qualifications and experience. Minimum Qualifications Earned doctorate in Counselor Education from a CACREP-accredited program.  ABD candidates will be considered if the degree will be conferred by August 1st, 2027.  Licensed as an LPC/LPCC/LMHC or license-eligible in the State of Minnesota. If not licensed, able to submit application for licensure upon the semester of hire. https://mn.gov/boards/behavioral-health/ or a licensed school counselor or license-eligible for a tier 3 or 4 license in MN upon the semester of hire. Counseling experience sufficient to teach and supervise master’s level counseling students. Evidence of professional identity as a counselor educator – for example, membership and service in ACA, ACES, or a division; supervision credentialing; or scholarship in counselor preparation. Demonstrated effectiveness in teaching, supervising, or mentoring students from a range of backgrounds and life experiences. Preferred Qualification  Clinical or scholarly expertise in any area that would extend our current faculty strengths – including, but not limited to, trauma and crisis response, addictions and co-occurring disorders, couples and family counseling, career development, group work, assessment, integrated behavioral health, rural mental health, school counseling, or counseling children and adolescents. Approved Clinical Supervisor (ACS) credential and/or equivalent supervision training and experience and/or board-approved supervisor status. Experience with CACREP self-study process, either in a self-study or as a site team reviewer. Certification or advanced training in a clinical/counseling modality (for example, Play Therapy, DBT, EMDR, TF-CBT, MI). An active scholarly agenda with evidence of, or clear potential for, peer-reviewed publication and presentation schedule. We do not expect any candidate to meet every preferred qualification.  If you meet the required qualifications and the position interests you, we want to hear from you.    Other Requirements We ask for three items now.  To reduce the burden of applying, an initial application consists of three items: 1. Curriculum Vitae: If ABD, including your scheduled defense date and      anticipated degree conferral date. 2. Cover Letter: describe your teaching, scholarship, counseling background,      and what draws you to this position. 3. Contact information for two (3) current, professional references.  Note: We       will not contact references without your permission. Candidates invited to a first-round interview will be asked for a teaching philosophy statement, a sample of scholarship or creative work, and unofficial graduate transcripts.  Official transcripts are required of finalists.  Please note that successful applicants must be eligible to work in the United States on or before the effective date of appointment. Eligibility includes being a U.S. citizen or national, a lawful permanent resident, or a foreign national authorized to work in the U.S. without the need for employer sponsorship. Employees must maintain work authorization without sponsorship for the duration of their appointment. Work Shift (Hours / Days of work) Please note that successful applicants must be eligible to work in the United States on or before the effective date of appointment. Eligibility includes being a U.S. citizen or national, a lawful permanent resident, or a foreign national authorized to work in the U.S. without the need for employer sponsorship. Employees must maintain work authorization without sponsorship for the duration of their appointment. Telework (Yes/No) Varies About St. Cloud State University serves close to 10,000 students and is committed to providing accessible, high-quality education that prepares graduates for meaningful careers and lifelong success. Located along the Mississippi River in St. Cloud, Minnesota, SCSU combines personalized support, applied learning, research, and community engagement to create opportunities for students, employees, and the region. With more than 133,000 alumni worldwide, SCSU continues to make a lasting impact through education, innovation, and service.   St. Cloud State University is committed to creating an inclusive and welcoming environment that values the unique contributions of all students, faculty, staff, and community members. We recognize that welcoming a variety of perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds strengthens our teaching, learning, research, and service mission. We encourage applications from individuals who share our commitment to student success, belonging, and excellence.   Employment for this position is covered by the collective bargaining agreement for the IFO which can be found at: https://www.ifo.org/ St. Cloud State University is committed to excellence and actively supports cultural diversity. To promote this endeavor, we invite individuals who contribute to such diversity to apply, including minorities, women, LGBTQIA+, persons with disabilities and veterans. St. Cloud State University does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, color, creed, religion, age, national origin, disability, marital status, status with regards to public assistance, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, or status as a U.S. veteran. The Interim Title IX coordinator at St. Cloud State University is Andrea Rooney. For additional information, contact the Office of Institutional Equity & Access, (320) 308-5123, Admin. Services Bldg. Rm 121  Salary is commensurate with experience and qualifications and is subject to the terms and conditions of the applicable bargaining unit agreement or personnel plan. Benefits Information: At Minnesota State, we take your well-being and work-life balance seriously. We offer a comprehensive benefits package that features low-cost medical and dental insurance with affordable deductibles, employer-paid life insurance, short- and long-term disability, and pre-tax healthcare and dependent care accounts with a roll-over option. To further support your holistic health, employees also have access to a confidential Employee Assistance Program (EAP) for mental health, legal, and financial counseling. Detailed plan details, rates, and coverage choices can be reviewed directly via the State Employee Group Insurance Program (SEGIP) website.   To support your life outside of work, our package includes 12 paid holidays, generous vacation and sick time, and six weeks of Paid Parental Leave. We also help you secure your financial future with a robust Pension Plan, 457(b) and 403(b) retirement accounts, and various investment options. Furthermore, we invest in your growth by providing dedicated professional development and tuition waivers for both you and your dependents. Please note that specific benefit eligibility and coverage levels may vary depending on the type of position, employee classification, and collective bargaining agreements. Desired Start Date: 08-16-2027 Position End Date: Open Date: 10-12-2026 Close Date: 11-30-2026 Posting Contact Name: Courtney Marie Graber Posting Contact Email: courtney.graber@stcloudstate.edu"
    },
    "https://minnstate.wd115.myworkdayjobs.com/Minnesota_State_Careers/job/St-Cloud/Assistant-or-Associate-Professor-of-Nursing--Full-Time-Probationary--Tenure-Track----Maternal-and-Child-Health-Focus-State-University-Faculty_JR0000005794": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor of Nursing, Full-Time Probationary (Tenure Track) - Maternal and Child Health Focus-State University Faculty",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nFull time\n\nAll Job Postings will close at 12:01 a.m. CT on the specified Closing Date (if designated). Working Title: Assistant or Associate Professor of Nursing, Full-Time Probationary (Tenure Track) - Maternal and Child Health Focus-State University Faculty Institution: St. Cloud State University Classification Title: State University Faculty Bargaining Unit / Union: 209: Inter Faculty Organization City: St. Cloud FLSA: Job Exempt Full Time / Part Time: Full time Employment Condition: Unclassified - Unlimited Academic Salary Range: $64,963.00 - $193,146.00 Job Description Join the Department of Nursing Science at St. Cloud State University   The Department of Nursing Science invites applications for a full-time, probationary (tenure-track) Assistant or Associate Professor of Nursing beginning January 2027. We seek a collaborative nurse educator committed to preparing practice-ready baccalaureate nurses and contributing to a student-centered academic community. The primary teaching need for this search is maternal and child health nursing; opportunities may also exist to teach across the undergraduate curriculum based on candidate expertise and departmental needs.    Candidates with a completed doctorate, those currently pursuing a doctorate, and master’s-prepared nurses interested in continued academic and professional development are encouraged to apply.    This is an in-person faculty position based on the St. Cloud State University campus in St. Cloud, Minnesota. Regular on-campus presence is required. The successful candidate will join faculty who value strong clinical partnerships, applied learning, clinical judgment, simulation, evidence-based practice, and preparation for contemporary nursing practice. Teaching assignments may include classroom, laboratory, simulation, clinical, hybrid, and online learning environments based on departmental and program needs.    St. Cloud State University is committed to effective teaching and student success. Faculty are expected to engage in ongoing professional development and, as appropriate to the appointment, scholarly achievement, continued preparation and study, student growth and development, and service to the university and community.  Department of Nursing Science: https://www.stcloudstate.edu/nursing/ Inter Faculty Organization collective bargaining agreement: https://www.ifo.org/  *The salary range is the contractual range; however, salary upon hire is dependent upon qualifications and experience. Position Overview   The Department of Nursing Science offers a pre-licensure Bachelor of Science in Nursing program and a fully online RN-BS Completion Program. The pre-licensure program is accredited by the Commission on Collegiate Nursing Education (CCNE) and approved by the Minnesota Board of Nursing. Educational and clinical experiences prepare graduates to provide person-centered nursing care in diverse and rapidly changing health care environments.  The department emphasizes clinical judgment, evidence-based practice, quality and safety, interprofessional collaboration, population health, professional identity, and preparation for the NCLEX-RN. Students learn through classroom, clinical, simulation, laboratory, and technology-enhanced experiences, supported by the Jim and Ann Marie Maciej Nursing Education Suite and strong regional clinical partnerships.  Primary Responsibilities   • Teach undergraduate nursing courses in classroom, clinical, simulation, and/or online environments, with a primary anticipated focus in general acute care nursing.  • Support student learning, clinical judgment development, professional formation, and progression toward entry-level nursing practice.  • Provide academic advising, mentoring, and guidance to undergraduate nursing students.  • Collaborate with nursing faculty, staff, adjunct clinical faculty, preceptors, and community/clinical partners.  • Participate in curriculum development, program evaluation, assessment of student learning, and continuous quality improvement.  • Contribute to accreditation, regulatory, governance, and departmental service activities.  • Engage in professional development, scholarly or creative activity, and service consistent with the expectations of a probationary faculty appointment and the IFO collective bargaining agreement.  • Participate in departmental initiatives related to contemporary nursing education, including simulation, clinical judgment, the AACN Essentials, NCLEX-RN, and Next Generation NCLEX (NGN), as appropriate.    Appointment at the Assistant or Associate Professor rank will be determined based on the successful candidate’s education, academic and professional experience, scholarly accomplishments, and qualifications consistent with St. Cloud State University and Minnesota State policies and the applicable collective bargaining agreement    Why Join SCSU Nursing?   This position offers an opportunity to help educate the next generation of nurses while building an academic career within a collaborative department. Faculty benefit from strong relationships with regional health care and community partners, opportunities to teach across multiple learning environments, and meaningful engagement in curriculum innovation, simulation, clinical judgment, student mentoring, and professional service. The department especially welcomes experienced clinicians who are interested in bringing their practice expertise into nursing education.  Minimum Qualifications Master’s degree in nursing (MSN) from an accredited institution completed by the date of appointment.  Current, unencumbered Registered Nurse (RN) license in the United States and eligibility for Minnesota RN licensure by the date of appointment.  Minimum of two years of professional nursing practice experience.  Clinical knowledge or professional experience relevant to maternal and child health nursing and/or a related area of nursing practice.  Demonstrated potential for effective teaching, clinical instruction, precepting, mentoring, staff development, or professional nursing education.  Effective oral, written, interpersonal, and collaborative communication skills.  Demonstrated ability to work effectively with students, colleagues, clinical partners, and individuals from diverse backgrounds.  Commitment to student success and preparation of nurses for contemporary professional practice.  Preferred Qualifications Doctoral degree in nursing or a related field (PhD, DNP, EdD), completed or in progress.  Graduate preparation, certification, or substantial clinical expertise in maternal-newborn, women’s health, pediatric, family, or related nursing practice.  Teaching experience in a baccalaureate nursing program or other higher-education setting.  Experience teaching in clinical, simulation, skills laboratory, online, hybrid, or technology-enhanced learning environments.  Experience mentoring or precepting nursing students, new graduate nurses, or practicing nurses.  Familiarity with contemporary nursing education frameworks and standards, including the AACN Essentials, clinical judgment, NCLEX-RN, and Next Generation NCLEX (NGN).  Evidence of scholarly activity or demonstrated potential for scholarship appropriate to the faculty role.  Experience with curriculum development, program assessment, accreditation, regulatory review, or academic service.  Demonstrated interest in continued professional development and advancement as a nurse educator. Documents Needed to Apply Current curriculum vitae or resume.  Cover letter addressing the minimum qualifications and describing the applicant’s interest in the position, relevant nursing practice experience, teaching/mentoring experience or potential, and general acute care or related specialty expertise.  Contact information for three professional references who can address the applicant’s clinical expertise, teaching/mentoring effectiveness or potential, collaboration, and professional qualifications.  Unofficial graduate transcripts. Official transcripts will be required if the candidate advances in the hiring process Other Requirements Please note that successful applicants must be eligible to work in the United States on or before the effective date of appointment. Eligibility includes being a U.S. citizen or national, a lawful permanent resident, or a foreign national authorized to work in the U.S. without the need for employer sponsorship. Employees must maintain work authorization without sponsorship for the duration of their appointment. Work Shift (Hours / Days of work) Varies About St. Cloud State University serves close to 10,000 students and is committed to providing accessible, high-quality education that prepares graduates for meaningful careers and lifelong success. Located along the Mississippi River in St. Cloud, Minnesota, SCSU combines personalized support, applied learning, research, and community engagement to create opportunities for students, employees, and the region. With more than 133,000 alumni worldwide, SCSU continues to make a lasting impact through education, innovation, and service. St. Cloud State University is committed to creating an inclusive and welcoming environment that values the unique contributions of all students, faculty, staff, and community members. We recognize that welcoming a variety of perspectives, experiences, and backgrounds strengthens our teaching, learning, research, and service mission. We encourage applications from individuals who share our commitment to student success, belonging, and excellence. Employment for this position is covered by the collective bargaining agreement for IFO which can be found at: https://www.ifo.org/ St. Cloud State University is committed to excellence and actively supports cultural diversity. To promote this endeavor, we invite individuals who contribute to such diversity to apply, including minorities, women, LGBTQIA+, persons with disabilities and veterans. St. Cloud State University does not discriminate on the basis of race, sex, color, creed, religion, age, national origin, disability, marital status, status with regards to public assistance, sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, or status as a U.S. veteran. The Interim Title IX coordinator at St. Cloud State University is Andrea Rooney. For additional information, contact the Office of Institutional Equity & Access, (320) 308-5123, Admin. Services Bldg. Rm 121  Salary is commensurate with experience and qualifications and is subject to the terms and conditions of the applicable bargaining unit agreement or personnel plan. Benefits Information: At Minnesota State, we take your well-being and work-life balance seriously. We offer a comprehensive benefits package that features low-cost medical and dental insurance with affordable deductibles, employer-paid life insurance, short- and long-term disability, and pre-tax healthcare and dependent care accounts with a roll-over option. To further support your holistic health, employees also have access to a confidential Employee Assistance Program (EAP) for mental health, legal, and financial counseling. Detailed plan details, rates, and coverage choices can be reviewed directly via the State Employee Group Insurance Program (SEGIP) website.   To support your life outside of work, our package includes 12 paid holidays, generous vacation and sick time, and six weeks of Paid Parental Leave. We also help you secure your financial future with a robust Pension Plan, 457(b) and 403(b) retirement accounts, and various investment options. Furthermore, we invest in your growth by providing dedicated professional development and tuition waivers for both you and your dependents. Please note that specific benefit eligibility and coverage levels may vary depending on the type of position, employee classification, and collective bargaining agreements. Desired Start Date: 01-05-2027 Position End Date: Open Date: 09-21-2026 Close Date: 10-21-2026 Posting Contact Name: Courtney Marie Graber Posting Contact Email: courtney.graber@stcloudstate.edu"
